#
# Tiles are created ahead of a fling, as far as it will go before they are
# needed (replaces Atlantic's directional coverage and speed ladder for the
# page's backing stores).
#
# WebKit creates a layer's tiles one ring at a time around the visible
# rect's centre: each rendering update adds the missing tiles nearest to it,
# behind and to the sides as much as ahead. During a fast fling on OSNews the
# main thread ran 26 rendering updates a second (each held back until the
# previous update's tiles were painted), so a ring per update (one 512 px row
# each way) kept up with about 5300 CSS px/s; faster flings ran onto
# unpainted tiles: 6-12% of the frames while coasting had more than 5% of the
# screen unpainted, some all of it.
#
# The backing store now tracks how fast its visible rect moves. While it
# moves faster than about half a screen a second:
# - the cover rect reaches 200 ms of travel ahead (between half a screen and
#   four), a quarter of a screen behind and nothing to the sides; the keep
#   rect also holds a screen behind, so a flick back finds its tiles;
# - distance is counted in rows ahead of the leading edge (tiles behind come
#   last), and a pass creates every tile the page will cross in about two
#   updates (60 ms of travel), not one ring.
# At rest WebKit's cover and order are unchanged.
# PACIFIC_TILE_VELOCITY_COVER=0 keeps WebKit's cover while moving.
#
